m length: 8 mm (Day 7, dark) \u2192 9 mm (Day 14, light). Growth occurred after light exposure.\n\n2.  **Dish B (Light for first 7 days, then dark for next 7 days):**\n    -   Germinated seeds: 20 (Day 7, light) \u2192 20 (Day 14, dark). Germination was high and stable.\n    -   Green-leaved seedlings: 15 (Day 7, light) \u2192 15 (Day 14, dark). Green-leaved seedlings were present and stable.\n    -   Yellow-leaved seedlings: 5 (Day 7, light) \u2192 5 (Day 14, dark). Yellow-leaved seedlings were present and stable.\n    -   Mean stem length: 3 mm (Day 7, light) \u2192 3 mm (Day 14, dark). Growth was minimal.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n-   The green-leaved seedlings only appeared in Dish A after exposure to light (Day 14). This suggests that green-leaved seedlings require light to develop or survive. In Dish B, they were present from the start (under light) and remained stable under dark conditions, which is unusual for many plants (which often need light for chlorophyll synthesis and growth). However, the key point is that green-leaved seedlings were not present in the dark (Dish A, Day 7), indicating they are light-dependent for development.\n\n-   The yellow-leaved seedlings were present in Dish A even in the dark (Day 7) and were also present in Dish B under light. This suggests that yellow-leaved seedlings can germinate and develop even in the absence of light. This is a key adaptation for germination in dark environments (like soil).\n\n-   The data shows that yellow-leaved seedlings germinated in the dark (Dish A, Day 7) and were able to survive and grow under light (Dish A, Day 14, and Dish B, Day 14). In contrast, green-leaved seedlings did not germinate or develop in the dark (Dish A, Day 7).\n\n**Evaluating the options:**\n\nA: **The yellow-leaved phenotype can germinate in environments without light.**  \nThis is directly supported by the data. In Dish A (which was dark for the first 7 days), yellow-leaved seedlings were present (12 of them), while green-leaved 